2026-09-06T00:00Z claude note Imagine phase (raid, sonnet): PROCEED, highest priority of the four Fable-findings tickets (the incident-linked one). Confirmed unchanged. Implementation guidance, traced against the real code and the existing test suite: gate `heartbeatTick` on a newly-exposed `state.busy` flag (set by the scheduler's own outer `finally`, mirroring how `busy` already works internally), NOT on `state.phase !== 'ready'` -- that alternative was tried and found to break the existing test `after a hang the ring names the active document, its last progress and every heartbeat` (tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs:333), because after `stop()` aborts a sweep mid-extraction, `state.phase` stays stale at 'extracting' (phase-fixup is skipped once `enabled=false`), so a phase-based guard would wrongly keep emitting. `busy` goes false in the outermost finally the instant sweep() truly exits, matching that test unmodified. Also null-guard `elapsedMS`/`sinceProgressMS` (`startedAt`/`lastProgressAt` may be stale/null during census). For journal survival: expose a second, never-deleted reference (e.g. `Zotero.SDTPackSitterJournal = journal`, set once alongside the existing `journal ??= createSDTJournal()`) rather than restructuring what shutdown() deletes -- the existing test asserting `Zotero.SDTPackSitter === undefined` post-shutdown names a different property and is unaffected. No line-level conflict with 0701/0702/0704 (touches only heartbeatTick()/shutdown()), but same files -- sequence via rebase. Priority nuance: the live incident (a stuck, actively re-running loop, not a dead one) matches 0701's unconditional re-census+rehash more than 0702's silent-death pattern -- fix 0703 first as the diagnostic instrument, but don't let it crowd out 0701 as equally causally relevant.
--- body ---
## Context

Fable 5.1 deep-QA pass (2026-09-06). Ticket 0689's `heartbeatTick` returns
immediately whenever `state.active === null` -- which is the case for the
entire census phase, the whole `blocked()` admission check, and
`host.list()`. Hangs inside those phases (e.g. `IOUtils.stat`/`exists` on
a dead network mount, a stalled synchronous `/proc` read in `blocked()`)
leave no heartbeat record and no other trace after the last `cache-load`
event -- 0689's stated goal ("diagnosable after a hang") does not hold
for the phase where the author's own hang this morning ("stuck in a
census loop") most plausibly occurred, especially combined with ticket
0701's finding that census re-hashes the whole library on every sweep.

Also noted: after a disable, `delete Zotero.SDTPackSitter` makes the ring
buffer unreachable from outside, and `Zotero.debug` output only survives
if debug logging was already enabled before the hang -- so the natural
recovery action (disable/re-enable) destroys the evidence.

## Work

- Emit a heartbeat (or an equivalent periodic "still alive, currently in
  phase X" record) whenever the sitter is doing *any* work -- census,
  admission checks, extraction -- not only while a document is actively
  extracting.
- Keep a reference to the most recent journal reachable after a disable
  (e.g. a module-level last-session snapshot), so disabling to recover
  from a hang doesn't erase the only evidence of what hung.

## Acceptance

- [ ] A synthetic hang inside census (not extraction) produces at least
      one heartbeat-equivalent record naming the census phase before the
      hang.
- [ ] The journal from the most recent session remains readable
      immediately after a disable, before any re-enable.
